('1ce4a5c0-0000-4000-8000-000000001137', '1ce4a5c0-0000-4000-8000-000000000031', '1ce4a5c0-0000-4000-8000-000000000011', null, 'inbound', $str$Hi Yashwant ,
Remember the issue that I was facing with the event sequence? Editing the publish date is not a clean way and it is too much to track back every time. I just asked Claude, and it gave me the following solution. Can you see if we can implement this, please? 
_____
Right now, events on the site display in order of WordPress "Publish Date" rather than the actual event date — that's why we've had to backdate the publish date as a workaround. Can you set it up so events sort automatically by their real date instead? Here's what's needed:
1. Install ACF (Advanced Custom Fields) — free version — if it's not already active on the site.
2. Create a new ACF field group attached to the Product post type, with a Date Picker field (e.g. meta key: event_date, saved in Ymd format). This is separate from the existing "Event Dates" custom attribute — keep that one as-is, it's just for the display text on the cards. The new field is purely to power sorting.
3. Go through the current/upcoming event products and fill in the real event date in this new field.
4. Find the widget powering the events grid on the events page (it's likely an Elementor Loop Grid or Products widget). Open its Content → Query settings and give it a Query ID — e.g. event-date-order.
5. Add this snippet (via the Snippets plugin already on the site) so that query sorts by the new field, soonest event first:
add_action('elementor/query/event-date-order', function($query) {
    $query->set('orderby', 'meta_value');
    $query->set('meta_key', 'event_date');
    $query->set('order', 'ASC');
});
6. Nice-to-have if you have time: use the same hook to auto-hide events once their date has passed (filter event_date >= today), so we don't have to manually trash old listings either.
